---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What is Meta Muse?',
    answer:
      'Meta Muse is a personal AI agent from Meta, released on September 8, 2026. It is designed to carry out tasks on web pages instead of only answering questions.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Meta Muse free?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Meta Muse has a free tier with a usage limit, along with paid plans priced at $20 per month and $100 per month. Our Meta Muse pricing guide covers the available tier information.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I use Meta Muse?',
    answer:
      'Install the Meta Muse app and sign in, choose the free tier or a paid plan, and connect Gmail or Google Calendar if your tasks require them. Our how to use Meta Muse guide walks through the setup steps.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Meta Muse the same as Meta AI?',
    answer:
      'No. Meta Muse is available as an app for iOS and Android and as a web product, separate from Meta AI.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Meta Muse available in my country?',
    answer:
      'The initial release is in the United States. Availability outside the United States is not covered by the verified details on this page.',
  },
];

const relatedLinks = [
  {
    label: 'what is Meta Muse',
    href: '/what-is-meta-muse/',
    description: 'A plain-language explanation of the product, its cloud computer, and how it differs from a chatbot.',
  },
  {
    label: 'how to use Meta Muse',
    href: '/how-to-use-meta-muse/',
    description: 'Setup steps for the app, plan selection, and Google service connections.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Meta Muse pricing',
    href: '/meta-muse-pricing/',
    description: 'What the free tier, the $20 plan, and the $100 plan mean for buyers.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Meta Muse review',
    href: '/meta-muse-review/',
    description: 'An early assessment focused on execution, privacy, and who should wait.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Meta Muse: What It Is, What It Costs, How to Use It"
  description="Meta Muse is Meta's personal AI agent that browses, books, and buys for you. Here's what it does, what the $20 and $100 plans include, and how to get started."
  canonicalPath="/"
  pageType="website"
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }]}
>
  <article class="content">
    <p class="eyebrow">Independent guide</p>
    <h1>Meta Muse Explained</h1>

    <section aria-labelledby="definition-heading">
      <h2 id="definition-heading">What Meta Muse Is</h2>
      <p>
        Meta Muse is a personal AI agent from Meta, released on September 8, 2026. It is built
        for everyday consumers and works on web-based tasks rather than stopping at a written
        answer. The practical goal is to hand over an outcome, then review the action it took or
        the result it produced.
      </p>
      <p>
        That distinction matters because a conversational tool usually returns instructions you
        carry out yourself. Meta Muse is positioned as an agent that can act on your behalf in a
        separate cloud environment. It can connect to some services and use a browser to work
        through a task, while keeping that work away from your own computer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="capabilities-heading">
      <h2 id="capabilities-heading">What Meta Muse Can Do</h2>
      <p>
        The currently listed capabilities center on routine online work. Each task still depends
        on access to the relevant website or service and on the details you provide.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Browse webpages:</strong> Open pages, gather information, and move through a
          site while working toward a requested result.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fill in forms:</strong> Enter details into fields on a website when a form is
          part of the task.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Buy products:</strong> Complete a purchase on a shopping website when given the
          product and purchasing details.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Order food:</strong> Place an order through a restaurant page rather than only
          returning dining recommendations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Send email:</strong> Write and send messages through a connected Gmail account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use Gmail and Google Calendar:</strong> Read email and check calendar
          information that is needed to complete a task.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These abilities describe the kinds of actions Meta says the product can take. They do not
        mean that every site or task will work without limits. A task can still fail when a page
        changes, a service restricts access, or the agent does not have enough information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="cost-heading">
      <h2 id="cost-heading">How Much Meta Muse Costs</h2>
      <p>
        Meta Muse has a free tier with a usage limit and two paid options. Meta says users who
        reach the free limit can upgrade or wait for the limit to refresh. The available details do
        not identify feature or allowance differences among the paid tiers.
        <!-- TODO: verify paid-plan feature and allowance differences. -->
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Meta Muse plans and their general fit</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Plan</th>
              <th scope="col">Price</th>
              <th scope="col">Best for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Free</th>
              <td>$0 listed</td>
              <td>Testing the agent and handling occasional tasks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Paid tier</th>
              <td>$20 per month</td>
              <td>Regular use that fits the lower paid tier.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Higher paid tier</th>
              <td>$100 per month</td>
              <td>Frequent use as an ongoing task tool.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        See the full breakdown on our <a href="/meta-muse-pricing/">meta muse pricing</a> page.
        Before paying, confirm the current plan details and service terms for your account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="start-heading">
      <h2 id="start-heading">How to Get Started</h2>
      <p>
        Setup begins with an account and a device. Meta Muse is available through separate iOS
        and Android apps and a web product, outside the Meta AI app experience.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Download the app and sign in.</strong> Choose the iOS or Android app, or use the
          web version, then sign in with your account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Choose the free tier or a paid plan.</strong> Start with the option that matches
          how often you expect to hand off online tasks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connect Gmail or Google Calendar.</strong> Add only the services a task needs,
          because those connections determine what information the agent can use.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For a fuller walkthrough, read our <a href="/how-to-use-meta-muse/">how to use Meta Muse</a> guide.
        The initial release is in the United States.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
      <h2 id="privacy-heading">Privacy and Trust</h2>
      <p>
        Media reports say Meta Muse has read user private messages, and privacy is the largest
        issue in the current discussion. That reported behavior deserves attention because an
        agent may need access to personal communications and connected services to complete a
        task. The concern is not limited to where the work happens; it also includes what data the
        system can see, use, or retain.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meta Muse runs in a separate cloud virtual machine called Muse Secure VM. The listed
        environment provides the storage, CPU, and memory needed for real work, and it includes
        its own browser with isolation from the user's local computer. That isolation can reduce direct exposure
        of your device, but it does not settle the privacy questions raised by the product's data
        access. Read our <a href="/meta-muse-review/">Meta Muse review</a> for a closer look at
        those tradeoffs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <FAQ items={faqItems} />

    <RelatedLinks links={relatedLinks} />
  </article>
</Base>
